'''1950–1955''': Wards 10 to 18 and 28 of the county borough of Stoke on Trent.<ref>{{Cite legislation UK |type=act |year=1948 |chapter=65 |act=Representation of the People Act 1948 |schedule=1 |accessdate=23 July 2023 }}</ref>

'''1955–1983''': Wards 9 to 16 of the county borough of Stoke on Trent.<ref>{{cite book |author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |date=1956 |title=Statutory Instruments 1955 |series=Part II |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8TDyAAAAMAAJ |chapter=The Parliamentary Constituencies (Stoke on Trent) Order 1955. SI 1955/172 |location=London |publisher=[[Her Majesty's Stationery Office]] |pages=2170–2171 |isbn=}}</ref>

'''1983–2010''': The Abbey, Berryhill, Brookhouse, Hanley Green, Hartshill, Shelton and Stoke West wards of the City of Stoke-on-Trent.<ref>{{cite legislation UK|type=si|si=The Parliamentary Constituencies (England) Order 1983|year=1983|number=417|accessdate=23 July 2023}}</ref>

'''2010–2024''': 

Since the implementation of the [[Fifth Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]] after the [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005 election]] the seat has had these electoral wards:
*[[Abbey Green ward|Abbey Green]], [[Bentilee]] and Townsend, Berryhill and [[Hanley, Staffordshire|Hanley East]], [[Hanley, Staffordshire|Hanley West]] and [[Shelton, Staffordshire|Shelton]], [[Hartshill, Staffordshire|Hartshill]] and [[Penkhull]], Northwood and Birches Head, and [[Stoke-upon-Trent|Stoke]] and Trent Vale ''in the [[Stoke-on-Trent City Council|City of Stoke-on-Trent]]''.<ref>{{cite news| title=New seat: Stoke-on-Trent Central| url=http://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/cgi-bin/calcwork.pl?seat=Stoke-on-Trent%20Central| work=Electoral Calculus| access-date=22 September 2008| url-status=dead| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110527105510/http://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/cgi-bin/calcwork.pl?seat=Stoke-on-Trent%20Central| archive-date=27 May 2011| df=dmy-all}}</ref><ref>{{cite legislation UK|type=si|year=2007|number=1681|si=The Parliamentary Constituencies (England) Order 2007|accessdate=23 July 2023}}</ref>

'''2024-present''':

Further to the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], enacted by the Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023, from the [[2024 United Kingdom general election]], the constituency is composed of the following (as they existed on 1 December 2020):

* The City of Stoke-on-Trent wards of: Abbey Hulton and Townsend; Bentilee and Ubberley; Birches Head and Central Forest Park; Boothen and Oak Hill; Eaton Park; Etruria and Hanley; Fenton East; Fenton West and Mount Pleasant; Hanley Park and Shelton; Hartshill and Basford; Joiner’s Square; Meir Hay; Penkhull and Stoke; Sandford Hill; Sneyd Green; Springfields and Trent Vale.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule 1 Part 8 West Midlands region}}</ref>

''In order to bring the electorate within the permitted range, significant parts of the [[Stoke-on-Trent South (UK Parliament constituency)|Stoke-on-Trent South]] constituency will be transferred in, including the town of [[Fenton, Staffordshire|Fenton]]. In addition, the boundary with [[Stoke-on-Trent North (UK Parliament constituency)|Stoke-on-Trent North]] will be re-aligned to take account of changes to ward boundaries.''

Following a further local government boundary review in the City of Stoke-on-Trent which came into effect in May 2023,<ref>{{Cite web |last=LGBCE |title=Stoke-on-Trent {{!}} LGBCE |url=https://www.lgbce.org.uk/all-reviews/stoke-trent |access-date=2024-04-10 |website=www.lgbce.org.uk |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=The Stoke-on-Trent (Electoral Changes) Order 2022 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2022/665/contents/made}}</ref> the constituency will now comprise the following from the 2024 general election:

* The City of Stoke-on-Trent wards of: Abbey Hulton; Basford & Hartshill; Bentilee, Ubberley & Townsend; Birches Head & Northwood; Boothen; Etruria and Hanley (nearly all); Bucknall & Eaton Park; Fenton East; Fenton West & Mount Pleasant; Hanley Park, Joiner’s Square & Shelton; Hartshill Park & Stoke; Meir Hay North, Parkhall & Weston Coney (part); Moorcroft & Sneyd Green (part); Penkhull & Springfields; Sandford Hill; Trent Vale & Oak Hill; and a very small part of Longton & Meir Hay South.<ref>{{Cite web |title=New Seat Details - Stoke-on-Trent Central |url=https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/calcwork23.py?seat=Stoke-on-Trent+Central |access-date=2024-04-10 |website=www.electoralcalculus.co.uk}}</ref>

==Constituency Profile==
The contribution of the city to Britain's economy and history is prominent as home to [[Staffordshire Potteries]]: [[Aynsley China|Aynsley]], [[Burleigh Pottery|Burleigh]], [[Royal Doulton|Doulton]], [[Dudson]], [[Heron Cross Pottery|Heron Cross]], [[Mintons|Minton]], [[Moorcroft]], [[Twyford Bathrooms|Twyford]] and [[Wedgwood]], most in this particular seat.

Owing to a reduction in clay and coal excavation works in the area, and canal trade, this seat has the highest unemployment rates of the three Stoke seats; this seat has 6.2% of workless registered unemployment benefit claimants, compared to a national average of 3.8% and regional average of 4.7%.<ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/news/datablog/2010/nov/17/unemployment-and-employment-statistics-economics Unemployment claimants by constituency] ''[[The Guardian]]''</ref>

==History==
The constituency was created for the [[1950 United Kingdom general election|1950 general election]]. The large town had, in succession, two forerunners, the first of which gained representation by way of the [[Reform Act 1832|"Great Reform Act"]] in 1832. The constituency has a majority of residents from a clear-cut working-class background, many of whom work or have worked in [[Trade unions in the United Kingdom|trade union]]-represented industries. Of these, many were employed in [[The Potteries]], the smaller foundries or in nearby hubs for the civil infrastructure and automotive industries; the latter of these remains an important source of employment in the region. The constituency's housing—overwhelmingly [[low-rise]], and in some cases highly ornate Victorian terraces and semi-detached houses—is, compared with [[Staffordshire]] as a whole, a relatively dense urban network of streets.

===Political history===
Statistics are confusing as to the current status of the area, reflecting the great demographic—in particular, economic sector—changes in the constituency. [[Tristram Hunt]]'s result at the [[2015 United Kingdom general election|2015 general election]] gave the seat the 60th-most marginal majority of the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]]'s 232 seats, measured by percentage of majority.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ukpolitical.info/labour-mps-elected-2015.htm |title=Labour Members of Parliament 2015 |website=UK Political.info |archive-date=2018-09-29 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180929214847/http://www.ukpolitical.info/labour-mps-elected-2015.htm |url-status=live}}</ref> Labour's continuous tenure of Stoke-on-Trent Central from 1950 to 2019 placed it among the approximately 120 constituencies of the 232 which returned Labour members in 2015—all their predecessor areas included—to have withstood landslides for the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] during the intervening period (such as the [[1983 United Kingdom general election|1983 general election]]). Stoke-on-Trent Central ranked highest for [[political apathy]] at the 2015 election, recording the lowest turnout in the United Kingdom.<ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2016/jan/15/stoke-on-trent-microcosm-labour-soul-jeremy-corbyn Why Stoke-on-Trent is a microcosm of the battle for Labour's soul] ''[[The Guardian]]'', 15 January 2016. Retrieved 13 January 2017.</ref>

===Prominent members===
[[Barnett Stross]] was awarded the Order of the White Lion in recognition of his role in the development of relations between the UK and [[Czechoslovakia]], and for his role in the renewal of the substantial village of [[Lidice]]; he also led statutory efforts that began specific protection of workers against industrial disease.

[[Mark Fisher (politician)|Mark Fisher]] was for 13 months the [[Minister for the Arts (United Kingdom)|Minister for the Arts]] at the outset of the [[First Blair ministry|Blair ministry]].

[[Tristram Hunt]] was the [[Shadow Secretary of State for Education]] in the Labour Party's [[Official Opposition Shadow Cabinet (United Kingdom)|Shadow Cabinet]] from October 2013 until September 2015.

