'''Wyre Forest''' is a [[county constituency]] represented in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom]].   It elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) by the [[first past the post]] system of election. The current MP is [[Mark Garnier]] of the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] who was  re-elected in the [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general election]].

== Boundaries ==
'''1983–1997''': The District of Wyre Forest.

'''1997–2010''': All the wards of the District of Wyre Forest except the Rock and Ribbesford ward.

'''2010–present''': The District of Wyre Forest.

The Wyre Forest constituency as it was drawn for the 1997 election was almost coterminous with the [[Wyre Forest (district)|Wyre Forest]] [[Districts of England|district]], with around 2,000 extra electors from the district in the neighbouring [[Leominster (UK Parliament constituency)|Leominster constituency]]. Following its review of parliamentary constituencies for the 2010 election, the [[Boundary Commissions (United Kingdom)|Boundary Commission]] recommended that the portions of the district currently in Leominster move into this seat, making the constituency and district wholly coterminous. These changes were brought about in part by the consideration of Worcestershire and Herefordshire separately for the drawing of parliamentary constituency boundaries.

== History ==
The Wyre Forest constituency was first fought under its present name in the [[1983 United Kingdom general election|1983 general election]], having succeeded the old [[Kidderminster (UK Parliament constituency)|Kidderminster seat]], and was won for the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]] by [[Esmond Bulmer]]. Wyre Forest was held with relative ease by the Conservatives' [[Anthony Coombs (politician)|Anthony Coombs]] in the [[1987 United Kingdom general election|1987]] and [[1992 United Kingdom general election|1992 general elections]]; however the [[1997 United Kingdom general election|1997]] [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] landslide saw that party gain the seat with [[David Lock]] securing a majority of almost 7,000. Anger over the downgrading of [[Kidderminster Hospital]] reflected on Lock at the [[2001 United Kingdom general election|2001 election]], and [[Independent Kidderminster Hospital and Health Concern|Health Concern's]] [[Richard Taylor (British politician)|Richard Taylor]] stormed to a decisive victory, then held the seat with a considerably reduced majority in 2005. Taylor lost to the Conservatives in the 2010 general election.<ref>[http://www.kidderminstershuttle.co.uk/news/local/8152591.Tory_hails__fresh_start__for_Wyre_Forest/ Tory hails 'fresh start' for Wyre Forest] - ''Kidderminster Shuttle''</ref>

Turnout in the Wyre Forest at general elections has generally been around 2-3% above the national average, but in 2001 the constituency recorded a 68.0% turnout as against 59.4% nationally; this spike (not repeated in 2005) is widely attributed to the intense local feelings on the hospital issue.

