'''Coventry North''' was a [[United Kingdom constituencies|parliamentary constituency]] in the city of [[Coventry]] in the [[West Midlands (region)|West Midlands]].  It returned one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) to the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom]], elected by the [[first past the post]] system.

== History ==

The constituency was created for the [[1950 United Kingdom general election|1950 general election]], and abolished for the [[February 1974 United Kingdom general election|February 1974 general election]].

Throughout its history this constituency provided a reasonably safe seat for Labour backbencher, Maurice Edelman. His majority ranged between 1,241 (2.8%) in the [[1959 United Kingdom general election|1959 election]] to 11,117 (22.2%) in [[1950 United Kingdom general election|1950]].

== Boundaries ==
The County Borough of Coventry wards of Bablake, Foleshill, Holbrook, Radford, and Sherbourne.

The constituency was one of three divisions of [[Coventry]], a [[parliamentary borough]] in the historic county of [[Warwickshire]] in the [[West Midlands (region)|West Midlands]] region of [[England]].

When Coventry was first divided for parliamentary purposes, in the 1945-50 Parliament, the area that later became this constituency was split between the then two seats of [[Coventry East (UK Parliament constituency)|Coventry East]] and [[Coventry West (UK Parliament constituency)|Coventry West]].

In 1974 Coventry was split into four new constituencies, and this seat disappeared. Foleshill ward became part of [[Coventry North East (UK Parliament constituency)|Coventry North East]], while the remaining four wards from this seat formed the whole of [[Coventry North West (UK Parliament constituency)|Coventry North West]].

