'''Birmingham Selly Oak''' is a [[List of United Kingdom Parliament constituencies|constituency]]{{#tag:ref|A [[borough constituency]] (for the purposes of election expenses and type of returning officer)|group= n}} in the [[West Midlands (county)|West Midlands]], represented in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]] since [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024]] by [[Alistair Carns]] of the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]].{{#tag:ref|As with all constituencies, the constituency elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) by the [[first past the post]] system of election at least every five years.|group= n}}

==Boundaries==
The seat includes many [[University student|students]] and [[University lecturer|staff]] from the nearby [[University of Birmingham]]. Half of the university's Selly Oak campus is located within the constituency. The [[Cadbury UK|Cadbury]] factory and [[Cadbury World]] are also within its boundaries.

'''1955–1974''': The County Borough of Birmingham wards of [[Balsall Heath]], Moseley and King's Heath, and Selly Oak.<ref>{{cite book |author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8TDyAAAAMAAJ |title=Statutory Instruments 1955 |date=1956 |publisher=[[Her Majesty's Stationery Office]] |isbn= |series=Part II |location=London |pages=2099–2102 |chapter=The Parliamentary Constituencies (Birmingham and North Warwickshire) Order 1955. SI 1955/177}}</ref>

'''1974–1983''': The County Borough of Birmingham wards of King's Norton, Moseley, and Selly Oak.

'''1983–2010''': The City of Birmingham wards of Bournville, [[Kings Norton|King's Norton]], [[Moseley]], and Selly Oak.

'''2010–2024''': The City of Birmingham wards of [[Billesley, West Midlands|Billesley]], [[Bournville]], [[Brandwood (ward)|Brandwood]], and [[Selly Oak (ward)|Selly Oak]].

'''2024–present''': The City of Birmingham wards of Billesley; Bournbrook & Selly Park; Bournville & Cotteridge; Brandwood & King’s Heath (part); Druids Heath & Monyhull; Highter’s Heath; Stirchley; Weoley & Selly Oak (part).<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule 1 Part 8 West Midlands region}}</ref>

Minor differences reflecting the revised ward structure in the City of Birmingham which became effective in May 2018.<ref>{{Cite web |last=LGBCE |title=Birmingham {{!}} LGBCE |url=https://www.lgbce.org.uk/all-reviews/birmingham |access-date=2024-02-29 |website=www.lgbce.org.uk |language=en}}</ref>

==History==
Since its creation in 1955 the seat has switched hands three times between [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] and the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]]. The seat has progressively swung towards Labour from being a safe Conservative seat; this has been attributed to housing redevelopments that took place in the 1960s and 1970s.{{citation needed|date=December 2012}} More recently, it has acquired a reputation for electing outspoken MPs, first with the victory of [[Anthony Beaumont-Dark]] of the Conservatives in 1979, and then with his defeat by [[Lynne Jones]] of the Labour Party in 1992.{{citation needed|date=February 2024}}

